.TH "UBERTOOTH\-EGO" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-ego\fR \- Yuneec E\-Go sniffing
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-f
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
The Yuneec E\-Go is an electric skateboard controlled by a wireless handheld remote\. This handheld remote uses a custom protocol implemented in 2\.4 GHz RF\. ubertooth\-ego(1) can sniff this protocol and interfere with its operation\.
.P
Each run captures in the mode it asks for, even on a board left in another E\-GO mode by a run that was killed rather than interrupted: the next run takes that board over and starts its own mode, on the channel and with the access code it states\. See ubertooth\-util(1), CARRY\-OVER BETWEEN RUNS\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Sniff the connection between an E\-Go and its remote:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-f
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Receive all E\-Go packets on 2408 MHz:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-r \-c 2408
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Interfere with a connection:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-i
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "OPTIONS"
Major modes:
.TP
\fB\-f\fR
Sniff a connection by hopping along channels
.TP
\fB\-r\fR
Continuously receive packets on a single channel (see \fB\-c\fR)
.TP
\fB\-i\fR
Interfere with a connection
.TP
\fB\-j\fR
Original "jam" spelling of \fB\-i\fR; does exactly what \fB\-i\fR does
.P
Options:
.TP
\fB\-c <2402\-2480>\fR
Channel in MHz to use for continuous receive (suggested: 2408, 2418, 2423, 2469; default: 2418)\. Only \fB\-r\fR uses it: \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-i\fR and \fB\-j\fR hop across the E\-Go's own channel list, so \fB\-c\fR with one of those is refused rather than ignored\. The default is sent like any other channel, so \fBubertooth\-ego \-r\fR on its own receives on 2418 MHz rather than on whatever channel the board was left on\.
.TP
\fB\-l <1\-48>\fR
How much of each packet to keep, in bytes (default: 18)\. The value must be a complete decimal integer from 1 through 48; invalid values fail before the Ubertooth is opened\. Only the modes that read a packet use it, so \fB\-l\fR with \fB\-i\fR or \fB\-j\fR is refused rather than ignored: interference waits for the sync word and transmits\. The default is sent in every mode, so what a later receiving run inherits from the board is the same either way\.
.TP
\fB\-a <access_code>\fR
Access code to use, given as 2, 4, 6 or 8 hex digits (1 to 4 whole bytes)\. A code shorter than four bytes becomes the most significant bytes of the radio's 32\-bit sync word and the rest are zero, so \fB\-a 1122\fR matches \fB11220000\fR, not a shorter pattern\. See ACCESS CODE below for what a run without \fB\-a\fR uses\.
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
print the usage text on standard output and exit 0\. It takes effect once the whole command line has parsed, so a bad option value, a stray word or an option given twice is still reported\.
.SH "ACCESS CODE"
\fB\-a\fR sets the access code for the run that asks for it\. A run without \fB\-a\fR receives with \fB630f9ffe\fR: a mode start puts the code back to that default, so one run's \fB\-a\fR does not reach the next\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-ego \-r \-c 2418 \-a 11223344   # receives with 11223344
ubertooth\-ego \-r \-c 2418               # receives with 630f9ffe
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The code is stated just after the request that starts the mode, which is what keeps a board left in another mode from throwing it away as it leaves it; see ubertooth\-util(1), CARRY\-OVER BETWEEN RUNS\. Continuous receive tunes once, so a code that arrives after the capture has started costs its first millisecond, while \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-i\fR and \fB\-j\fR pick one up on their next hop\. All three modes use the access code\.
.P
A program driving the board by hand can still set a code of its own and have the capture that follows use it, as it can with a channel: a mode started on an idle board resets nothing, because no earlier mode's code can be there to reset\.
.SH "TRANSMIT POWER"
Interference (\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-j\fR) transmits at the CC2400 power level set with \fBubertooth\-util \-a\fR and leaves it unchanged\. A board that has not been given a level jams at 7 (0 dBm, the maximum), the CC2400's reset value and the level jamming always used before\. Sniffing does not transmit and does not change the level\. These levels are at the CC2400 radio; the Ubertooth One's RF amplifier changes the actual output after that\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-ego\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: channel must be 2402\-2480 MHz (got 2481)\fR\.
.P
A major mode is the work, so a command line without \fB\-f\fR, \fB\-r\fR or \fB\-i\fR is refused before any device is opened: \fBerror: nothing to do: choose a major mode (\-f, \-r or \-i)\fR\. It used to open the selected board, check its firmware, do nothing and exit 0\.
.P
Each major mode runs until it is interrupted, so only one of them can run: two in one command are refused before any device is opened, naming both as they were spelled, \fBerror: \-r cannot be combined with \-f; choose one of \-f, \-r or \-i\fR\. \fB\-i\fR and \fB\-j\fR are two spellings of the jamming mode, so they clash too\. The last mode written used to be the one that ran, and the others were dropped without a word\.
.P
\fB\-c\fR outside continuous receive is refused before any device is opened too, naming the mode as it was spelled: \fBerror: \-c cannot be combined with \-f; only continuous receive (\-r) uses a channel\fR, with no usage text after it (see DIAGNOSTICS)\. It used to be accepted and then dropped silently in those modes\.
.P
\fB\-l\fR with interference is refused the same way: \fBerror: \-l cannot be combined with \-i; only the receiving modes (\-f, \-r) read a capture length\fR\. It used to set a firmware value the mode never reads\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
Standard output carries only the captured packets\. Errors, warnings and progress go to standard error, each error as one line starting \fBerror:\fR, for example \fBerror: E\-GO not supported by this firmware\fR\.
.P
A capture that is losing packets says so\. This tool reads its packets one at a time, and a reply that held part of a record cannot be read as one, so it is dropped and reported once per run: \fBwarning: a poll reply arrived with 32 of a packet's 64 bytes and was dropped; the capture is losing packets\fR\. The capture runs on and ends as it otherwise would, so the warning is the only sign\.
.P
A poll the board does not answer is tried again, up to three attempts, before the capture gives up: a board busy elsewhere for a moment no longer ends a capture that has been running for hours\. A board that has left the bus ends it at once\. The retry saves the capture, not the packet: the board takes a packet out of its queue as it answers the poll, so the one a timed\-out poll was carrying is gone\.
.P
Ctrl\-C is not made to wait for those attempts\. Once you have asked the run to stop, a poll that fails is not tried again: the capture ends there, and the reason the poll failed is reported, so a stop that lands on a board which was not answering exits 1 rather than 0\.
.P
A request that sets the capture up and is refused by the board ends the command there, named in one line, for example \fBerror: could not set the channel\fR; libubertooth has already said why\. A refused request is not proof that the board never got it, so the message does not claim one way or the other\.
.P
A command line the tool will not take is refused before any device is opened, with exit status 1 and one \fBerror:\fR line on standard error and nothing after it: an option the tool does not know (\fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR), one whose value is missing (\fBerror: option '\-c' requires a value\fR), a bad option value, a stray word, \fB\-c\fR outside continuous receive, \fB\-l\fR with interference, and a command line that said something but has nothing to do\. The line describes the problem, so the usage text is left to \fB\-h\fR\. A bare \fBubertooth\-ego\fR, with no arguments at all, is the one refusal the usage text does follow, on standard error with the \fBerror:\fR line\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR is what the user asked for, so the usage text goes to standard output and nothing goes to standard error\.
.SH "GAPS IN A CAPTURE"
A capture that is losing packets says so\. The board holds captured packets in a queue until this tool reads them, and a queue with no room left loses the next packet\. The first time it happens the capture warns \fBwarning: the board's packet queue filled up and it dropped at least one packet; this capture has a gap\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many such gaps there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 gaps where the board's queue filled up and it dropped packets\fR\. The count is of gaps, not of lost packets: one full queue can swallow several packets, and the board sends no number\. Nothing says which packets are missing, so a capture with gaps is complete only between them\. The board reports a gap on the first packet that gets through after it, so a capture that ends in the moment after a gap does not report that last one\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 when the command did what was asked, including \fB\-h\fR, and after a capture is interrupted (Ctrl\-C) having polled cleanly to that point\. A Ctrl\-C that lands on a poll the board did not answer is the exception: the attempts stop with it, the reason is reported, and the run exits 1, because the board had stopped answering\. 1 for a command line the tool will not take or with nothing to do, when the selected Ubertooth is not found or cannot be opened, when the board refuses one of the requests that set the capture up, when the firmware does not support the requested mode, and when the capture stops short because a poll failed\.
.P
1 as well when the board refuses the request that ends the capture: it is left in the E\-GO mode, and no other tool can open it until it is reset or re\-plugged\. libubertooth names the refused request on standard error\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR reads the attached board, so it exits 1 when a board is attached and cannot be read, or when the USB bus cannot be listed at all; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\.
.P
A run stopped by Ctrl\-C before it had opened a board exits 0 as well: see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE\.
